Adding some preliminary responses with a view on EU-wide Omnibus Directive. If local laws do not indicate otherwise, we may follow the guidelines below.



# end date of the campaign - is it a must in all countries to mention end date for the discount? {color:#ff5630}Omnibus Directive does not set campaign end date requirement for price reductions. Therefore, unless stated otherwise under the local laws, we shall not indicate the end date of the campaign.{color}
# exclusions - do we need to state all the products that are excluded from the campaign or there can be one generic legal phrase, like "some products might be excluded from discount" that will work in all markets? {color:#ff5630}Omnibus Directive does not set any requirements for exclusions from the campaigns. Therefore, if there are no further requirements under the local laws, your wording may be used to indicate exclusion of some goods.{color}
# push notifications - these are really short, do we need to mention all the details also in push message, e.g. the dates and the excluded products? {color:#ff5630}Again, no requirements to do so under Omnibus Directive. However, this practice may be considered as misleading trading technique. For example, if  push notification says ‘{color}{color:#ff5630}_discount on the whole menu -50%’_ {color}{color:#ff5630}but then the consumer intends to buy alcohol and finds out that alcohol item is not discounted, this may be considered as a misleading trading technique. In Estonia we are risking the fine of up to EUR 400,000. We may, of course, argue that the consumer was able to see that alcohol was not discounted before placing the purchase on the app, but this is not a bulletproof solution. If any consumer or the Consumer Protection Board would intend to make any claims with regard to that, we may face the fine.{color}

